      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Following a pure, strong state model, a policy that fails decisive border control would indeed be fundamentally and inherently \"ineffective\" because it would be seen as a political and sovereign failure first and foremost. In this rigid view, all other policy outcomes, including beneficial economic data, are subordinate to the absolute assertion of state power over its physical domain.\n\nThis perspective frames the \"win condition\" of national policy overwhelmingly in the terms of absolute control and the political will to enforce that control unequivocally.",
      "source": "target",
